clojure.data.json — JSON parser and generator in pure Clojure (Stuart Sierra and contributors), ported to this
runtime for make corpus-bench (docs/notes/benchmarks.md, "Corpus workloads")

Repository: https://github.com/clojure/data.json
Version:    2.5.2, the Maven Central jar org/clojure/data.json/2.5.2/data.json-2.5.2.jar (sha1
            76b861b2dcb06e2b59a008303bea53d1d15c1a53, its pom's scm tag v2.5.2)
License:    Eclipse Public License 1.0 (LICENSE)

File taken, from the jar: clojure/data/json.clj -> src/clojure/data/json.clj

What is ported, and why. data.json's reader and writer are Clojure over java.io and java.lang: a definterface
stream with a mutable position, char[] windows, StringBuilder, StringWriter, JVM exceptions and protocol extensions
to JVM classes. Every change is a `pippin:` comment at its place, and pippin.diff is the whole diff against the
jar's file. read-str and write-str behave as upstream on every input the workload gives them (its result is the
JVM's, checked with = on every run); what behaves otherwise, or not at all, is named below.

- InternalPBR is a protocol (definterface is refused, design §8), its call sites functions in place of method
  calls. StringPBR keeps its position in its ^:unsynchronized-mutable ^long field, as upstream, reads a char with
  nth and counts the string with count: indexes are code points here, UTF-16 units on the JVM, which
  differ only outside the BMP. readChars and unreadChars move the position without a char[] buffer, and
  read-quoted-string scans its 64-char window in place and takes the string with subs, where upstream copies it
  into a char[] and makes a String of it.
- StringBuilder and StringWriter are a string in a volatile, which (vswap! v str x) appends to in place while the
  volatile alone holds it (string-builder, append, builder-str; NOTES "Strings"); .append of a range is subs.
- Exception. and EOFException. are ex-info with the same message (json-error); (catch NumberFormatException ...)
  is unchanged. Long/valueOf, Double/valueOf, Integer/parseInt, Integer/toHexString and Math/min are this runtime's
  statics (docs/jvm-differences.md).
- The JSONWriter extensions: Boolean, Long for every integer box, Double, the ratio, bigint and decimal types taken
  from values, UUID, Keyword and Symbol for clojure.lang.Named, String for CharSequence, IPersistentMap for
  java.util.Map and IPersistentCollection for java.util.Collection (a map is found as a map first), Object.
  Float, the atomics, java.time.Instant, java.util.Date and java.sql.Date go with their writers and the
  :date-formatter and :sql-date-converter options: an #inst is written by :default-write-fn. write-generic asks
  for the array type where upstream asks (class x) .isArray (class is refused, design §8). instance? of
  clojure.lang.Named in default-write-key-fn is keyword? or symbol?.
- Not carried: read and write (a java.io.Reader and Writer), ReaderPBR, pprint (clojure.pprint), write-json,
  print-json and pprint-json; read-json takes a String only; on-extra-throw-remaining takes the stream's rest as a
  string, which is what toReader returns here. The two (set! *warn-on-reflection* true) forms go (design §8).

What differs from upstream: a \u escape of a surrogate half throws, since (char n) of one does (a char is a
Unicode scalar here), so a character outside the BMP must arrive unescaped; and with :escape-unicode such a
character is written as one \u escape of five or six hex digits, which is not JSON. The workload's text is BMP
only. There is no manifest.edn: this directory is not a corpus library with a suite of its own, and
bench/workloads/data-json/manifest.edn is its root for corpus-bench's clj-facts.
